\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{The domain-wall operator}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{On an oriented periodic square lattice with normal period at least #{2} and positive transverse period, let #{A} and #{B} be complementary nonempty strips perpendicular to the first axis. A real mass function takes the value #{M_A} on #{A} and #{M_B} on #{B}. Each positive step carries its own oriented scalar link #{u_j(z)}, including a loop at transverse period one. Fields take values in #{S\otimes F}, for nonzero finite Hermitian spin and fiber spaces as in the \vocabk{Wilson kernel}{fdip-0153}. The supplied self-adjoint Clifford directions #{E_j=\varepsilon_j^S\otimes\bbI_F} obey #{E_iE_j+E_jE_i=2\delta_{ij}\bbI}, and #{\Gamma} is a constant self-adjoint involution anticommuting with them. Set #{P_{j,\pm}=(\bbI\pm E_j)/2}. The unitary shift is #{(T_j^u\psi)(z)=u_j(z)^{-1}\psi(z+e_j)}.}

\p{At spacing #{a>0} the \newvocab{domain-wall operator} is #{H_{\rm DW}=\Gamma K}. With Wilson coefficient #{a/2} its two directed hops in each coordinate give
##{aK=(2-aM(\cdot))\bbI -
\sum_{j=1}^2\left(P_{j,-}T_j^u+P_{j,+}(T_j^u)^\dagger\right).}
This formula uses the \vocabk{Wilson operator}{fdip-009U} with the mass subtracted at each site. The grading exchanges the two projectors and commutes with the shifts and real mass, so #{H_{\rm DW}} is self-adjoint. Its \newvocab{wall half-signature} is
##{h(H_{\rm DW})=\tfrac12(\nu_-(H_{\rm DW})-\nu_+(H_{\rm DW})).}
Here #{\nu_+} and #{\nu_-} are the positive and negative subspace dimensions. The operator is regular when it is invertible, as in the constant-mass comparison of \ref{fdip-0155}.}

\p{Assign each positively oriented plaquette to the strip of its base site. Write #{F_{12}(x,y)} for the scalar product around its oriented boundary, using the single-line \vocabk{plaquette angle}{fdip-015V}. Require the phase to differ from #{-1}, and take its principal angle with magnitude less than #{\pi}. The \newvocab{strip flux} of #{A} is the real number
##{w_A=\frac1{2\pi}\sum_{(x,y)\in A}\arg F_{12}(x,y).}
Use the same convention on #{B}. The \newvocab{wall neighborhood} of radius #{R}, for an integer #{R\ge0}, consists of all sites within graph distance #{R} of either endpoint column of either strip, together with all links incident to those sites.}
